1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,813
If your nonprofit takes federal money, one rulebook covers all of it.

2
00:00:04,813 --> 00:00:07,115
It's called the Uniform Guidance.

3
00:00:07,115 --> 00:00:10,115
The official name is two C F R two hundred.

4
00:00:10,115 --> 00:00:11,929
Most people never read it.

5
00:00:11,929 --> 00:00:15,277
They just hear the word compliance and tense up.

6
00:00:15,277 --> 00:00:18,137
Today I'll explain what's actually in it.

7
00:00:18,137 --> 00:00:21,765
Plain English, about seven minutes, no lawyer-speak.

8
00:00:21,765 --> 00:00:26,648
And I'll show you the four numbers that changed in twenty twenty-four.

9
00:00:26,648 --> 00:00:29,927
A lot of advice online still uses the old ones.

10
00:00:29,927 --> 00:00:32,438
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

11
00:00:32,438 --> 00:00:34,810
I build grant compliance software.

12
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:39,065
To build it, I had to learn exactly what these rules require.

13
00:00:39,065 --> 00:00:42,274
I'm not an auditor, and I won't talk like one.

14
00:00:42,274 --> 00:00:45,970
You're getting the version a busy director actually needs.

15
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:46,915
So what is it?

16
00:00:46,915 --> 00:00:50,693
The Uniform Guidance is one rulebook for federal grants.

17
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:54,066
The federal budget office, called O M B, wrote it.

18
00:00:54,066 --> 00:00:57,845
It covers grants and the agreements that come with them.

19
00:00:57,845 --> 00:01:01,016
Back in twenty thirteen, the rules were a mess.

20
00:01:01,016 --> 00:01:03,445
There were eight separate rulebooks.

21
00:01:03,445 --> 00:01:08,370
Which one applied depended on who you were and what kind of cost you had.

22
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:11,541
The Uniform Guidance pulled all eight into one.

23
00:01:11,541 --> 00:01:14,173
People nicknamed it the Super Circular.

24
00:01:14,173 --> 00:01:16,467
It took effect in twenty fourteen.

25
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:18,423
And it doesn't just bind you.

26
00:01:18,423 --> 00:01:21,729
It binds the federal agency that gives the money.

27
00:01:21,729 --> 00:01:26,722
It binds any group in the middle that passes the money down, like a state.

28
00:01:26,722 --> 00:01:29,893
And it binds anyone you hand the money to next.

29
00:01:29,893 --> 00:01:31,500
Same rulebook, top to bottom.

30
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,026
The rulebook is long.

31
00:01:33,026 --> 00:01:36,006
But you really live in three parts of it.

32
00:01:36,006 --> 00:01:38,913
The first part is how you run the grant.

33
00:01:38,913 --> 00:01:40,003
The day to day.

34
00:01:40,003 --> 00:01:44,145
How you buy things, how you keep records, how you report.

35
00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:48,651
The second part is what you're allowed to charge to the grant.

36
00:01:48,651 --> 00:01:50,322
This is the money part.

37
00:01:50,322 --> 00:01:52,357
It trips people up the most.

38
00:01:52,357 --> 00:01:55,192
The third part is who checks your work.

39
00:01:55,192 --> 00:01:56,427
That's the audit.

40
00:01:56,427 --> 00:01:58,389
We'll get to it at the end.

41
00:01:58,389 --> 00:02:00,279
Run it, cost it, prove it.

42
00:02:00,279 --> 00:02:05,670
Keep those three jobs straight and you understand the shape of the whole thing.

43
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:10,280
Let's do the money part, because it's the one you touch every day.

44
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:15,309
Before you charge a cost to a federal grant, it has to pass three words.

45
00:02:15,309 --> 00:02:16,077
Reasonable.

46
00:02:16,077 --> 00:02:16,776
Allocable.

47
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:17,474
Allowable.

48
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:21,805
Reasonable means a careful person would spend about that much.

49
00:02:21,805 --> 00:02:27,253
Allocable means the cost really belongs to this grant, not some other program.

50
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:30,187
Allowable means the rules don't forbid it.

51
00:02:30,187 --> 00:02:33,959
Some things, like alcohol or lobbying, are simply out.

52
00:02:33,959 --> 00:02:35,914
There are two kinds of cost.

53
00:02:35,914 --> 00:02:40,804
Direct costs you can tie to one project, like a program staffer's pay.

54
00:02:40,804 --> 00:02:45,763
Indirect costs keep the whole place running, like rent and bookkeeping.

55
00:02:45,763 --> 00:02:48,068
You can't pin those to one grant.

56
00:02:48,068 --> 00:02:51,002
So you recover them with an indirect rate.

57
00:02:51,002 --> 00:02:52,259
Hold that thought.

58
00:02:52,259 --> 00:02:55,960
The indirect rate is one of the numbers that just changed.

59
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:02,955
In twenty twenty-four, O M B rewrote the rulebook in plainer language and raised a bunch of thresholds.

60
00:03:02,955 --> 00:03:08,931
Most of them kick in for awards given out on or after October first, twenty twenty-four.

61
00:03:08,931 --> 00:03:11,783
Here are the four that matter most to you.

62
00:03:11,783 --> 00:03:12,054
One.

63
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:18,709
If you don't have a negotiated rate, you can now charge an indirect rate of up to fifteen percent.

64
00:03:18,709 --> 00:03:19,932
It used to be ten.

65
00:03:19,932 --> 00:03:25,093
That's real money back for overhead, and you don't have to negotiate for it.

66
00:03:25,093 --> 00:03:25,365
Two.

67
00:03:25,365 --> 00:03:31,205
The line for what counts as equipment went from five thousand dollars to ten thousand.

68
00:03:31,205 --> 00:03:36,909
So something that costs, say, eight thousand dollars used to be tracked as equipment

69
00:03:36,909 --> 00:03:39,014
with all the rules that brings.

70
00:03:39,014 --> 00:03:40,576
Now it's just a supply.

71
00:03:40,576 --> 00:03:41,595
Less paperwork.

72
00:03:41,595 --> 00:03:42,002
Three.

73
00:03:42,002 --> 00:03:44,379
When you pass money to a subgrantee

74
00:03:44,379 --> 00:03:50,015
the first fifty thousand dollars of that subaward counts toward your indirect base.

75
00:03:50,015 --> 00:03:52,732
It used to be only twenty-five thousand.

76
00:03:52,732 --> 00:03:57,282
So you can recover a bit more overhead on your bigger partnerships.

77
00:03:57,282 --> 00:03:57,621
Four.

78
00:03:57,621 --> 00:03:58,436
The big one.

79
00:03:58,436 --> 00:04:04,130
The single audit threshold went from seven hundred fifty thousand dollars to one million.

80
00:04:04,130 --> 00:04:05,866
So what's a single audit?

81
00:04:05,866 --> 00:04:11,352
It's one outside audit of your whole group, not a separate audit of each grant.

82
00:04:11,352 --> 00:04:13,852
One audit covers everything federal.

83
00:04:13,852 --> 00:04:15,172
Here's the trigger.

84
00:04:15,172 --> 00:04:21,352
If you spend one million dollars or more in federal money in a single year, you need one.

85
00:04:21,352 --> 00:04:22,880
Notice the word spend.

86
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:27,949
It's about what you actually spent that year, not what you were promised.

87
00:04:27,949 --> 00:04:30,311
This one follows your fiscal year.

88
00:04:30,311 --> 00:04:36,144
It applies to fiscal years that start on or after October first, twenty twenty-four.

89
00:04:36,144 --> 00:04:39,894
A single audit has to be done by an independent C P A.

90
00:04:39,894 --> 00:04:45,936
They follow the government's audit standards and file the result in a federal database.

91
00:04:45,936 --> 00:04:47,741
It's a real piece of work.

92
00:04:47,741 --> 00:04:50,033
The line moved up to one million.

93
00:04:50,033 --> 00:04:53,780
So some smaller groups that used to need one don't anymore.

94
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:55,847
So that's the Uniform Guidance.

95
00:04:55,847 --> 00:04:57,915
One rulebook for federal money.

96
00:04:57,915 --> 00:04:58,648
Three jobs.

97
00:04:58,648 --> 00:05:02,449
Run the grant, charge it right, and prove it in an audit.

98
00:05:02,449 --> 00:05:07,318
And as of twenty twenty-four, four of the numbers moved up in your favor.

99
00:05:07,318 --> 00:05:09,785
You don't need a consultant to start.

100
00:05:09,785 --> 00:05:13,119
You need to know which rules apply to your grants.

101
00:05:13,119 --> 00:05:16,387
And you need a system that keeps the proof ready.

102
00:05:16,387 --> 00:05:19,988
That's the kind of thing we built GrantPipe to handle.

103
00:05:19,988 --> 00:05:22,989
But the knowledge matters more than any tool.

104
00:05:22,989 --> 00:05:28,258
If you want a simple place to start, we made a free Grant Compliance Checklist.

105
00:05:28,258 --> 00:05:30,792
It covers the basics so nothing slips.

106
00:05:30,792 --> 00:05:32,659
We'll send it to your inbox.

107
00:05:32,659 --> 00:05:35,660
Thanks for watching, and good luck out there.
